name: variables
boss: $VARIABLE
flavor: I hold what you've forgotten.
syntax: ;, NAME=, ${#NAME}, ${NAME:-WORD}, ${NAME#%}, ${NAME}, $(CMD), `CMD`, "..."
brief: A variable is NAME=VALUE, with no spaces around the =. You read it back
brief: with $NAME. Command substitution $(CMD) runs a command and becomes its
brief: output, which is how you get a number out of wc and into a comparison.
brief:
brief: Quote your expansions. "$x" is one word whatever spaces it holds; unquoted,
brief: $x splits on them and any * left is matched against the directory. A
brief: backslash protects the next character. Most shell bugs are a missing quote.
brief:
brief: ${NAME} is the same expansion with braces, so you can glue text right
brief: after a name. ${#NAME} is its length, ${NAME:-WORD} falls back to WORD
brief: when NAME is unset or empty.
brief:
brief: A variable lives in the shell that set it. export hands it down to the
brief: programs that shell starts; env prints what is being handed over, and
brief: sh -c 'echo $x' proves which of the two you made. unset removes a
brief: variable, unalias an alias.
brief:
brief: ; runs the next command whatever the last one did. && only runs it if
brief: the last one succeeded.
---
q: {store the word @word@ in a variable called @var@|put @word@ into a variable named @var@|make a variable called @var@ hold the word @word@}
a: @var@=@word@
why: conceptual
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: no spaces around the equals sign
---
q: {set @var@ to @word@, then show its value|assign @word@ to @var@, then print it back|store @word@ in @var@, then show what @var@ holds}, with echo
a: @var@=@word@; echo $@var@
out: @word@
ref: echo
teach: echo
hint: $NAME (or ${NAME}) is how you read a variable back
---
q: {grab the current timestamp into a variable called @tvar@ so you can reuse it|save right-now's date and time in @tvar@ before it changes|stash the current timestamp in @tvar@ for later}
a: @tvar@=$(date)
why: primitive-cost
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: $( ) runs a command and becomes its output
---
q: {stash @log@'s line count in @cvar@ to check again later|save how many lines @log@ has right now into @cvar@|record @log@'s current line count in @cvar@ for later}, with wc
a: @cvar@=$(wc -l < @log@)
why: primitive-cost
ref: wc
teach: wc -l
hint: command substitution feeds wc's count straight into the assignment
---
q: put the word @word@ in @var@, then %show% how many characters it holds
a: @var@=@word@; echo ${#@var@}
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: a hash inside the braces gives the length
---
q: %show% @var@, or the word default if it is unset or empty
a: echo ${@var@:-default}
out: default
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: :- substitutes default only when the variable is unset or empty
---
q: %show% @log@'s line count as "lines: N", with echo
a: echo "lines: $(wc -l < @log@)"
out: ^lines: [0-9]+$
ref: echo
teach: echo
hint: $( ) expands fine inside double quotes, right beside literal text
---
q: {export @mvar@ with the value @word@ so child processes inherit it|set @mvar@ to @word@ and export it so children see it|make @mvar@=@word@ part of the environment, not just this shell}
a: export @mvar@=@word@
why: primitive-cost
ref: export
teach: export
hint: export is what makes a variable visible to child processes
---
q: %show% every exported variable in the environment
a: env
cmp: ref
ref: env
teach: env
hint: env with no arguments dumps the whole environment
---
q: {run one command with @mvar@ set to @word@, shell unchanged|run a single command that sees @mvar@=@word@, shell untouched|give just one command @mvar@=@word@ without exporting it}, with env
a: env @mvar@=@word@ sh -c 'echo $@mvar@'
out: @word@
ref: env
teach: env
hint: env VAR=value cmd sets it for that command alone, not your shell
---
q: {empty the environment first, then print empty to prove it|run with no inherited environment, printing empty to prove it|strip the environment to nothing, then echo empty to prove it}, with env
a: env -i sh -c 'echo empty'
out: empty
ref: env
teach: env
hint: -i drops the entire inherited environment before running the command
---
q: {remove @mvar@ from the environment, printing gone to prove it|run a command that cannot see @mvar@, printing gone to prove it|hide @mvar@ from one command, printing gone to prove it}, with env
a: env -u @mvar@ sh -c 'echo gone'
out: gone
cmp: ref
ref: env
teach: env -u
hint: -u strips one variable out of the environment for that command only
---
q: %show% the environment NUL-separated so xargs -0 can parse it safely
a: env -0
cmp: ref
ref: env
teach: env -0
hint: -0 separates entries with NUL instead of a newline
---
q: {remove the variable @var@ from your shell entirely|delete @var@ so it is no longer set at all|make @var@ fully unset, not just empty}
a: unset @var@
why: primitive-cost
ref: unset
teach: unset
hint: unset removes the variable entirely, unlike setting it to an empty string
---
q: {read a line from standard input into a variable called @var@|take one line of input and store it in @var@|capture the next line typed into a variable named @var@}
a: read @var@
why: interactive
ref: read
teach: read
hint: read takes one line from stdin and stores it in the named variable
---
q: {read a raw path into @var@ so backslashes survive intact|read user input into @var@ without losing any backslashes|take a line into @var@ exactly as typed, backslashes and all}, with read
a: read -r @var@
why: interactive
ref: read
teach: read -r
hint: -r is what you want essentially always; without it a backslash vanishes
---
q: {prompt with "name: " and read the reply into @var@|show "name: " then read what comes back into @var@|ask "name: " and store the answer in @var@}, with read
a: read -p "name: " @var@
why: interactive
ref: read
teach: read -p
hint: -p prints its argument as a prompt before reading
---
q: set d to a comma, then %show% @csv@'s first column with cut
a: d=,; cut -d"$d" -f1 @csv@
out: ^gateway$
ref: cut
teach: cut -d
hint: the variable holding the separator still needs quoting when it's used
---
q: {spawn a one-off shell that just echoes the word @word@|run a throwaway shell whose only job is to echo the word @word@|fire off a fresh shell to print the word @word@, nothing else}
a: sh -c 'echo @word@'
out: ^@word@$
ref: sh
teach: sh -c
hint: -c takes the command to run as its next argument
---
q: {remove every alias at once|clear out all aliases in one command|wipe every alias you have defined}, with unalias
a: unalias -a
why: conceptual
ref: unalias
teach: unalias -a
hint: -a clears every alias in one shot
---
q: {check what ls actually is before you trust it in a script|find out in plain english what running ls would do|make sure ls isn't secretly an alias or function}, with command
a: command -V ls
cmp: ref
ref: command
teach: command -V
hint: -V gives a full descriptive sentence rather than just a path
---
setup: your PATH might be hijacked or broken
q: %list% @txt@ with ls run through command, standard PATH not yours
a: command -p ls @txt@
out: @txt@
cmp: ref
ref: command
teach: command -p
hint: -p ignores your PATH and uses the system's standard one instead
---
q: %show% every place in your PATH with a command called sh, with which
a: which -a sh
cmp: ref
ref: which
teach: which -a
hint: -a lists every match in PATH, not just the first one found
---
q: set @var@ to @word@, then echo it with s glued on the end
a: @var@=@word@; echo ${@var@}s
cmp: ref
ref: echo
teach: echo
hint: without the braces the shell looks for a variable called @var@s instead
---
q: {put @txt@ in @var@, then %show% everything after its first dot|store @txt@ as @var@, then %show% just the part past the first dot|save @txt@ in @var@, then %show% only what comes after the dot}
a: @var@=@txt@; echo ${@var@#*.}
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: "#" strips the shortest match off the front -- *. eats through the first dot, leaving the rest
---
q: {put @csv@ in @var@, then %show% it with the .csv ending removed|store @csv@ as @var@, then %show% it without the .csv part|save @csv@ in @var@, then %show% just the name, no .csv}
a: @var@=@csv@; echo ${@var@%.csv}
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: "%" strips the shortest match off the end -- .csv has to match the literal ending
---
q: {put @ip@ in @var@, then %show% just its last octet|store @ip@ as @var@, then %show% only its last octet|save @ip@ in @var@, then %show% its last octet alone}
a: @var@=@ip@; echo ${@var@##*.}
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: "##" is the greedy version of "#" -- it eats through the LAST dot, not just the first
---
q: {put @ip@ in @var@, then %show% just its first octet|store @ip@ as @var@, then %show% only its first octet|save @ip@ in @var@, then %show% its first octet alone}
a: @var@=@ip@; echo ${@var@%%.*}
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: "%%" is the greedy version of "%" -- it eats from the FIRST dot onward, keeping only the start
---
q: {grab the current timestamp into @tvar@, but write it the older backtick way|save the current date/time into @tvar@ using backticks, not $( )|stash the timestamp in @tvar@ with backticks instead of $( )}
a: @tvar@=`date`
why: conceptual
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: backticks are the older command-substitution syntax -- $( ) reads better but scripts you'll read still use `cmd`
---
q: {give ls a shortcut called @word@, then list your aliases|make @word@ short for ls, then show your aliases|set @word@ as a nickname for ls, then list all aliases}, with alias
a: alias @word@=ls; alias
cmp: ref
ref: alias
teach: alias
hint: alias with no name or value lists every alias you've defined, as name=value
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: f=app.log; echo ${f%.log}
a: app
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: % strips a suffix off the value, leaving what came before it
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: f=app.log; echo ${f#*.}
a: log
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: # strips a prefix instead, so *. eats everything up to the first dot
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: echo ${u:-fallback}
a: fallback
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: u was never set, so the value after :- is used instead
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: x=foo.bar.baz; echo ${x#*.}
a: bar.baz
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: # strips the shortest match off the front -- *. stops at the first dot
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: x=foo.bar.baz; echo ${x##*.}
a: baz
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: ## is greedy -- *. now stretches to the LAST dot, taking everything before it
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: x=foo.bar.baz; echo ${x%.*}
a: foo.bar
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: % strips the shortest match off the end -- .* stops at the last dot
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: x=foo.bar.baz; echo ${x%%.*}
a: foo
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: %% is greedy -- .* now stretches back to the FIRST dot, taking everything after it
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: unset v; : ${v:=hello}; echo $v
a: hello
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: := doesn't just substitute a fallback like :- does -- it actually assigns it to v
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: v=x; echo ${v:+replaced}
a: replaced
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: :+ is the mirror of :- -- it substitutes its word when v IS set, ignoring v's own value entirely
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: v="a b"; printf '[%s]' $v; echo
a: [a][b]
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: unquoted $v splits on the space into two words -- printf's format recycles for each one
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: v="a b"; printf '[%s]' "$v"; echo
a: [a b]
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: quoted, "$v" stays one word, space and all -- printf's format runs just once
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: mkdir -p g1; cd g1; touch a1 a2; p='a*'; echo $p
a: a1 a2
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the quotes only held while p was being set -- unquoted, $p's value is globbed against the directory after it expands
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: mkdir -p g2; cd g2; touch a1 a2; p='a*'; echo "$p"
a: a*
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: quoted, the expanded value is never re-read as a glob -- it stays the two characters it is
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: v=hi; echo '$v'
a: $v
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: single quotes stop the shell reading the $ at all, so there is nothing left to expand
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: printf '[%s]' a\ b; echo
a: [a b]
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the backslash protects just the next character, so the space joins the two into one word
